<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">5 January in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 5 January, from 1066 to 1968.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1066">1066 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">960 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Edward the Confessor dies, leaving England's throne disputed</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">King Edward died at Westminster without children. Rival claims by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William of Normandy</a> led to two invasions and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> that autumn.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1477">1477 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">549 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Charles the Bold of Burgundy killed at the Battle of Nancy</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> died fighting Swiss and Lorrainer troops outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a>. His death ended Burgundian ambitions to become a kingdom, and his lands were divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1675">1675 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">351 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Turenne defeats the imperial and Brandenburg armies at Turckheim</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a>, the French army under Marshal Turenne made a surprise winter march through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> and defeated the imperial and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> forces near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a>. The victory drove the Germans back across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-turckheim">Battle of Turckheim</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1757">1757 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">269 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Robert-François Damiens attempts to assassinate Louis XV</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>, Damiens stabbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> with a penknife, causing only a slight wound. He was later executed with notorious cruelty, the last person in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to be put to death by quartering.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1895">1895 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">131 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Alfred Dreyfus is publicly stripped of his rank in Paris</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a>, wrongly convicted of treason in December 1894, was degraded at the Military School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>: his badges were torn off and his sword broken. Still protesting his innocence, he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devils-island">Devil's Island</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dreyfus-affair">Dreyfus Affair</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devils-island">Devil's Island</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1919">1919 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">107 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">German Workers' Party founded in Munich</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Railway locksmith Anton Drexler founded the small nationalist German Workers' Party. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> joined that autumn and in 1920 the party was renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">National Socialist German Workers' Party</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1933">1933 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">93 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Construction of the Golden Gate Bridge begins</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Work began on a bridge across the Golden Gate strait at the entrance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> Bay. Opened in 1937, its 1,280-metre main span was the longest in the world until 1964.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1968">1968 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">58 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Alexander Dubček takes charge in Czechoslovakia</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Dubček replaced Antonín Novotný as leader of the Czechoslovak Communist Party. His reforms, 'socialism with a human face', inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a>, ended by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> invasion in August.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a></div></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 5 January <span class="n">6</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/stephen-decatur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/stephen-decatur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Stephen Decatur</span><span class="tp">1779–1820</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/konrad-adenauer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Konrad Adenauer</span><span class="tp">1876–1967</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/edwin-barclay"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/edwin-barclay.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Edwin Barclay</span><span class="tp">1882–1955</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-durrenmatt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/friedrich-durrenmatt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Friedrich Dürrenmatt</span><span class="tp">1921–1990</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/juan-carlos-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/juan-carlos-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Juan Carlos I</span><span class="tp">b. 1938</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mansoor-ali-khan-pataudi"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Mansoor Ali Khan Pataudi</span><span class="tp">1941–2011</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 5 January <span class="n">6</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/edward-the-confessor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Edward the Confessor</span><span class="tp">c. 1003–1066</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iv-the-curly"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/boleslaw-iv-the-curly.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Bolesław IV the Curly</span><span class="tp">c. 1122–1173</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/charles-the-bold.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Charles the Bold</span><span class="tp">1433–1477</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/felix-manz"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Felix Manz</span><span class="tp">c. 1498–1527</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/catherine-de-medici.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Catherine de' Medici</span><span class="tp">1519–1589</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-radetzky-von-radetz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/joseph-radetzky-von-radetz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Joseph Radetzky von Radetz</span><span class="tp">1766–1858</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
